```cpp
// Copy up to MAX_CONFIG_LEN bytes from the data.
config_len = std::min(size, MAX_CONFIG_LEN);
memcpy(config, data, config_len);

// Parse configuration. Exit if the configuration was bad.
rc = parse_config(spell_config, config, config_len);
if (rc == -1) goto EXIT_LABEL;

// Move the data pointer past the config.
data_str += rc;
size -= rc;
```

So the config is parsed from the beginning of the data. The config lines are key=value pairs separated by newlines. An empty line (just `\n`) terminates the config. The remaining data is the document.

The config can set `encoding=ucs-2`. Then the document text is treated as ucs-2 encoded.

When `aspell_document_checker_process` is called with the document text, it processes it. Then `aspell_document_checker_next_misspelling` returns tokens with offsets and lengths into the original data.

When `aspell_speller_suggest` is called with `data_str + token.offset` and `token.len`, the suggest function eventually calls `Convert::convert` which calls `DecodeDirect<Uni16>::decode` with the word pointer and its length.

If the token length is odd (not a multiple of 2), the decode function will read past the buffer.

So the PoC needs:
1. Config that sets encoding to ucs-2 and sets up a language
2. Document text in ucs-2 encoding where a "word" has an odd byte length

